 left), T3(0), T4(1), T9(0). Only T4 maybe for T2-T3-T4 needs T3(0) so no. Or T8 pung possible (2 T8 left).  \n\nBest: aim for T8 pung: I have 1 T8, 2 left unseen. Keep T8, discard others except W3 pair.  \n\nBut T5 is safer to keep? T5 is middle of Bamboo, can pair with drawn T5 (2 left), or form chow with T4,T6 (T4:1 left, T6:0 left) \u2192 no.  \n\nActually W3: There are 3 W3 left besides my two, so possible W3 pung. Keep W3,W3, discard T2,T5,T8? But I must discard one now, then have 4 tiles, need to discard another after next draw? Wait, after this discard, I\u2019ll have 4 hand tiles, next turn draw gives 5, then discard to stay at 4. So I can aim: keep W3,W3,T5,T8 and hope to draw T4 or T6 or T8 or T5 or T9.  \n\nCheck meld possibility for Bamboo: T4,T5,T6 chow needs T4(1 left) and T6(0) \u2192 dead. T5,T6,T7 chow needs T6(0), T7(0) \u2192 dead. T6,T7,T8 chow needs T6(0), T7(0) \u2192 dead. T7,T8,T9 chow needs T7(0), T9(0) \u2192 dead. T8,T9,T?? only T8,T9 need T7 or T10 \u2192 dead. T8 can only make pung or pair. So Bamboo sequences almost impossible due to T6,T7,T9 all gone.  \n\nThus Bamboo tiles in my hand are bad for chows, maybe good for pung/ pair.  \n\nW suit: W3 I have 2, W3 left 3 in wall, so can pung. W3 chow: W1-W2-W3 or W2-W3-W4 or W3-W4-W5.  \nW1: 2 left, W2: 1 left, W4: 2 left, W5: 2 left.  \nW1-W2-W3: W1(2),W2(1),W3(3 left) \u2192 possible.  \nW2-W3-W4: W2(1),W3(3),W4(2) \u2192 possible.  \nW3-W4-W5: W3(3),W4(2),W5(2) \u2192 possible.  \n\nSo keeping W3 pair is good, then T5 and T8 are less useful.  \n\nBut T8: maybe form pair with drawn T8 then Seven Pairs? No, can't go Seven Pairs with exposed melds.  \n\nScoring potential: I have B8 pung (dragon? no), B5-B6-B7 chow, T7 kong (all simples except T7 is simple), no honors in hand. If I win with W3 pair and a W chow, I could have All Simples (2 points), maybe other small patterns.  \n\nHowever, look at prevalent wind: South (F2), seat wind: North (F4). I have none in hand.  \n\nPotential big pattern: Half Flush? I have Bamboo melds and T7 (Bamboo) kong, plus W in hand \u2014 would need all W and maybe honors, but I have B suit melds exposed, so can't be Half Flush (Half Flush = only one suit + honors). I have B and T suits exposed, so not Half Flush.  \n\nI should just aim for quick win with whatever meld possible.  \n\nEasiest route: try to complete W3 pung, or get W2 or W4 to complete chow with W3.  \n\nKeep W3,W3, discard T8 or T5 or T2? Which is safest?  \n\nFrom unshown tiles, T8: 2 left, T5: 2 left, T2: 1 left. Discarding T2 is safest because only 1 left, less chance someone needs it for chow (but T1,T3 gone, T4 one left \u2014 T2-T3-T4 needs T3 (0) dead, T1-T2-T3 needs T1(0) dead, so T2 is relatively safe).  \n\nAlso, game history: many T2,T5,T8 discarded already. T2 was discarded by me earlier, by others too.  \n\nChoosing discard:  \n\n1. **Discard T2**: keeps W3 pair, T5, T8. Then draw, hope to draw W2 or W4 for chow with W3, or W3 for pung.  \n2. **Discard T5**: keeps W3 pair, T2, T8. Then maybe draw T4 for T2-T3-T4 (impossible, T3 gone) or draw T8 for T8 pair.  \n3. **Discard T8**: keeps W3 pair, T2, T5. Then draw W2/W4.  \n4. **Discard W3**: reduces pair possibility.  \n\nDiscard W3 is bad because it\u2019s central tile and I have 2, can form pung.  \n\nAmong T2,T5,T8, discard T2 seems best because it\u2019s the only one with 1 left, less useful for others.  \n\n**Evaluation:** Keep W3 pair, aim for W chow or W pung, discard T2 as safest and least useful.  \n\n**Reason:** T2 cannot form chows due to missing T1/T3; T5 and T8 still have 2 unseen each and may be slightly more dangerous to discard; also keeping T5/T8 might pair with drawn tiles for possible pair flexibility. Discarding T2 now reduces hand clutter.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T2.  \n\nFinal:  \nEvaluation: Best to discard T2, keeping W3 pair and T5,T8 for potential pair/pung or to discard later if better draw comes.  \nReason: T2 is isolated with no chow potential due to dead neighboring tiles, least risky to discard.  \nAnswer: Play T2"}
